WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.250
Let's go next to taffin from California.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:06.390
That is not a name I've seen before, taffin.

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:07.390
Oh, no.

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:09.620
I don't know where my parents got their name
from.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:10.620
Interesting.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:11.620
You've never asked.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:15.850
I mean, actually, I do know where it's from,
but it's like such a silly story like.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:17.550
But Mario.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:23.150
No, but my question is, this is something
I'm kind of noticing and it's kind of like

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:27.720
a question it's raised in my head that I think
might actually have some implications for

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.230
the Republican Party.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:35.800
OK, but I recently saw this video of like
a bunch of like Trump supporters in Georgia,

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.329
and it was some like rally or protest or whatever.

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:42.460
And one of the guys got up and started shouting
about how they were going to destroy the Republican

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:44.329
Party for betraying them.

00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:45.329
Right.

00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:47.809
And this is something I'm starting to think
like see more and more.

00:00:47.809 --> 00:00:52.410
And I think it's more of a trend now where
I'm seeing more infighting in the Republican

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:58.890
Party rather than actually like like going
towards any, like Democrats and stuff.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:03.179
And looking back, I think one of the things
that made the Republican Party so strong was

00:01:03.179 --> 00:01:05.479
they're like coalition building.

00:01:05.479 --> 00:01:08.369
And what I'm thinking is that what do you
think?

00:01:08.369 --> 00:01:13.600
That there's a possibility that, like how
radicalized that the Republicans and Trump

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.720
have made the base could actually start like
making blowback against the Republicans less?

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.970
We're seeing more and more of a bunch of like
Republicans like obviously they've supported

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.759
some of this stuff, but they can't support
all of it.

00:01:26.759 --> 00:01:30.130
And the moment you know that, they go against
it, like we saw with Tucker Carlson, the people

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:33.380
sort of like slamming back against them.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:37.521
And do you think that could start making some,
like, blowback or repercussions to the Republican

00:01:37.521 --> 00:01:38.521
Party?

00:01:38.521 --> 00:01:41.079
I would love to think that the answer is yes.

00:01:41.079 --> 00:01:48.120
But I think the most likely scenario is that
once Trump is gone and Joe Biden is sworn

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:52.480
in, there's this phrase return to the mean.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:57.930
And I think that we are going to see Republicans
basically go back to where they were before

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:00.990
Trump, which is Democrats really are the enemy.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:06.061
You'll still have these fringes of the Republican
Party that will say the problem is the Republican

00:02:06.061 --> 00:02:09.259
Party isn't right enough or whatever the case
may be.

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:12.500
But I think for the most part, you'll see
Republicans going back to exactly what they

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:18.930
were, where you see the low tax pro-business
conservatives, allied with the evangelicals,

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:24.470
allied with the libertarians, all united that
Democrats are really bad people, really bad.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:26.819
They're socialists, communists, et cetera.

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:30.340
And I don't think that the fracturing of the
Republican Party that you're seeing right

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:31.720
now is going to persist.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:33.489
Now, I could be wrong.

00:02:33.489 --> 00:02:36.120
And if it does persist, that could be great
for the left.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.120
But I'm not optimistic.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.989
I think the Republican Party will go right
back to what they were before Donald Trump.

00:02:40.989 --> 00:02:42.260
Yeah, you're probably right.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:45.750
I just got excited seeing that in Georgia
because the runoff is coming out.

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:46.750
Yeah.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:53.141
I think I also saw people, some some Republicans
saying, oh, we need to punish the Republican

00:02:53.141 --> 00:02:56.141
Party by not voting at all in Georgia in the
short run.

00:02:56.141 --> 00:02:58.140
I thought it was going to, which sounds great.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:03.650
I mean, if I was ever going to vote like Democrat,
but I could see them, like, just not voting.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:04.650
Exactly.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:05.650
Exactly.

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:08.379
And I hope that happens because it could actually
I mean, the polls are really tight in both

00:03:08.379 --> 00:03:09.510
of those races.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.700
They're essentially statistical ties.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:18.480
If if a million more Democrats register and
a million Republicans decide not to vote or

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.590
something, you know, whatever numbers that
alone could flip the results and give Democrats

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:25.459
the Senate, which would just be amazing.

00:03:25.459 --> 00:03:27.209
No.

00:03:27.209 --> 00:03:30.500
So I guess at least in the short term, there's
some benefit to be made out of that.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:34.270
Yeah, I do think Republicans will go back
to what they were, but in the next two and

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:39.280
a half months, there may actually be an advantage
to be gained from from the chaos.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:40.540
And let's hope that that's the case.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:42.940
OK, well, thanks for taking my question, David.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:43.940
All right.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:44.940
